      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate markdown.\n\nThis is part 2 of 2, a fragment. It contains:\n\n1. A software identification table with software versions and CRC checksums for \"Legally relevant firmware\" — versions 01.05, 01.15, 01.16, 01.21, 01.23, 01.36, 01.38, 01.42, 01.44, 01.47 with CRC checksums.\n\n2. Table 2: General characteristics of the family of instruments – Full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL — meter sizes DN32-DN450 with Ø in- and outlet, flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n\n3. Certificate header repeated (NMi, OIML Certificate, The Netherlands, Number R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6, Project number 4086814, Page 5 of 7).\n\n4. Continuation table with DN500 and DN600 for full bore type.\n\n5. Notes about flow rates freely chosen.\n\n6. Table 3: Reduced bore type MUT2300 — DN50-DN600 with flow rates and ratio Q3/Q1.\n\n7. Notes again.\n\n8. Page 6 of 7 — Table 4: Indicating device characteristics for full bore type — meter size groups with indicating range and verification scale interval.\n\n9. Table 5: Indicating device characteristics for reduced bore type MUT2300.\n\n10. Page 7 of 7 — Certificate history: revisions table.\n\nSince this is a fragment (part 2 of 2), I should extract only what's in this fragment. But the certificate-level info appears here: Number R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6, Project number 4086814, page_total 7, member_state The Netherlands.\n\nCertificate number format: R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01. The scheme appears to be \"A\" (in the number: 2013-A-NL1). Let me parse: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" — R49 recommendation, 2013 edition, scheme A, NL1 (Netherlands issuing authority), 19.01 certificate number. Hmm, actually NMi format: R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 where A = scheme.\n\nRecommendation: R49 (water meters), edition... The R49 references \"OIML R49-1: 2013(E)\". So edition 2013. Hmm, the recommendation edition — R49/2013 suggests edition 2013.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" — full string. Maybe number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\", revision \"6\".\n\ndate_issued: Revision 6 date is 10 August 2026. The date issued for current revision would be 10 August 2026 (odd year but that's what's in the table). Hmm, that's probably a typo in the cert (2026 vs 2024?) but I should not fabricate — the markdown says \"10 August 2026\". I'll use that as the date_issued for revision 6.\n\noiml_issuer_id: NMi — not clearly given an ID like \"NL1\" — the number contains NL1. NMi is the issuer. oiml_issuer_id might be \"NL1\"? Not explicitly stated. I could omit or use \"NL1\". I'll omit to be safe, or actually the cert number includes NL1 which is the NMi identifier. I'll leave it out since not explicitly present. Actually, hmm — many NMi certs don't list an issuer ID explicitly. I'll omit.\n\nmember_state: The Netherlands.\n\npage_total: 7 (from \"Page 7 of 7\").\n\nproject_number: 4086814.\n\nissuing_authority: NMi — the fragment shows \"NMi\" but no address. Since this is a fragment, the issuing authority details might have been in part 1. The fragment shows \"NMi\" only. I could include name \"NMi\". But the instruction says \"Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\" NMi name is present. I'll include name: \"NMi\" minimally. Hmm, but including a partial issuing_authority with only name might be fine.\n\ncertified_type: from tables — \"Full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL\" and \"Reduced bore type MUT2300\". Category: \"Gas meter\"? No — R49 is water meters. Category per schema examples: \"Water meter\". The schema category examples include \"Measuring system\", \"Gas meter\", etc. R49 = Water meters. So category: \"Water meter\". Type designations: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300.\n\nmodel_family: family covers meter sizes DN32–DN600 across two bore types (full bore MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL, reduced bore MUT2300). Models: each DN size per type family.\n\nHow to structure? The models vary by meter size and bore type. I could create models like \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\", ..., \"MUT2300 DN50\", etc.\n\nmatrix_tables: I should preserve Table 2 (full bore), Table 3 (reduced bore), Table 4 (indicating device full bore), Table 5 (indicating device reduced bore), and the software table.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, diameter_inlet_outlet per model.\n\nLet me enumerate the full bore rows (Table 2 + continuation):\n\nFull bore (MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL):\n- DN32: Ø32, Q1=0.125, Q2=0.2, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, Q3/Q1=200\n- DN40: Ø40, Q1=0.2, Q2=0.32, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 200\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1=0.315, Q2=0.504, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 200\n- DN65: Ø65, Q1=0.5, Q2=0.8, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 200\n- DN80: Ø80, Q1=0.8, Q2=1.28, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 200\n- DN100: Ø100, Q1=1.25, Q2=2, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 200\n- DN125: Ø125, Q1=2, Q2=3.2, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 200\n- DN150: Ø150, Q1=3.15, Q2=5.04, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 200\n- DN200: Ø200, Q1=5, Q2=8, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 200\n- DN250: Ø250, Q1=8, Q2=12.8, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 200\n- DN300: Ø300, Q1=12.5, Q2=20, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 200\n- DN350: Ø350, Q1=15.625, Q2=25, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 160\n- DN400: Ø400, Q1=20, Q2=32, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 200\n- DN450: Ø450, Q1=31.5, Q2=50.4, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 200\n- DN500: Ø500, Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n- DN600: Ø600, Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n\nReduced bore (MUT2300):\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1=0.2, Q2=0.32, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 315\n- DN65: Ø65, Q1=0.315, Q2=0.504, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 315\n- DN80: Ø80, Q1=0.5, Q2=0.8, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 315\n- DN100: Ø100, Q1=0.8, Q2=1.28, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 315\n- DN125: Ø125, Q1=0.8, Q2=1.28, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 315\n- DN150: Ø150, Q1=2, Q2=3.2, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 315\n- DN200: Ø200, Q1=3.15, Q2=5.04, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 315\n- DN250: Ø250, Q1=5, Q2=8, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 200\n- DN300: Ø300, Q1=8, Q2=12.8, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 200\n- DN350: Ø350, Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160\n- DN400: Ø400, Q1=12.5, Q2=20, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 200\n- DN450: Ø450, Q1=20, Q2=32, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 200\n- DN500: Ø500, Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n- DN600: Ø600, Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n\nIndicating device (Table 4, full bore):\n- DN32: indicating range min 99 999 m³, VSI 0.0001 m³\n- DN40, DN50: 99 999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100: 999 999, 0.001\n- DN125, DN150: 999 999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9 999 999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9 999 999, 0.1\n\nIndicating device (Table 5, reduced bore MUT2300):\n- DN50: 99 999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 99 999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 999 999, 0.001\n- DN200: 999 999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400: 9 999 999, 0.01\n- DN450, DN500, DN600: 9 999 999, 0.1\n\nSoftware versions table:\n- 01.05 → CAA8A4C7\n- 01.15 → 6AA50C55\n- 01.16 → E93E3A1E\n- 01.21 → 79413617\n- 01.23 → E7DD52E4\n- 01.36 → E1A52981\n- 01.38 → C2641A99\n- 01.42 → 2B6CF4C7\n- 01.44 → D867EC79\n- 01.47 → 7144DCB7\n\nRevision history:\n- Initial, 24 May 2019, \"-\"\n- 1, 4 February 2021, \"Addition of MUT1000EL and full-bore sizes DN32 and DN40; Lowering pressure loss class of MUT2300\"\n- 2, 10 May 2022, \"Addition of electronic hardware module (Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output). Addition of new software.\"\n- 3, 7 July 2022, \"Software update\"\n- 4, 14 February 2023, \"Software update\"\n- 5, 22 September 2023, \"Update of electronic hardware module. software update\"\n- 6, 10 August 2026, \"Address change, addition of new sizes, increase of Q3 values, addition of new software and minor hardware change.\"\n\nRevision should be string: \"Initial\", \"1\", \"2\", etc.\n\nNow, how to structure the JSON given this is fragment 2 of 2. The instruction says \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema ... for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nCertificate-level info IS present (number, project_number, page_total, member_state). date_issued — revision 6's date 10 August 2026 is in the revision history; that would be the issuance date of this revision. I'll include date_issued: \"2026-08-10\". Hmm, that's likely a typo for 2024 in reality but I extract as-is. Actually, wait — should I? The fragment doesn't explicitly say \"date issued\". The revision table says revision 6 dated 10 August 2026. Since the certificate is revision 6, its issue date is that. I'll include it.\n\nModel family: family could be \"MUT\" family? The models: two bore types with sizes. I'll define models with model_id combining bore type/family and size, e.g.:\n\nFull bore models: \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" ... but model_id should be a model identifier. Maybe use \"DN32 (full bore)\" etc. Let me think about what's cleanest.\n\nOption: models list:\n- {\"model_id\": \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\", \"label\": \"Full bore DN32\", \"attributes\": {\"meter_size\": \"DN32\", \"bore_type\": \"full\"}}\n- ...\n- {\"model_id\": \"MUT2300 DN50\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore DN50\", ...}\n\nFor model_level characteristics, use these model_ids.\n\nThat gives 16 full-bore models + 14 reduced-bore models = 30 models. model_level entries: q1, q2, q3, q4 (unit m³/h), ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless), diameter_inlet_outlet (mm). That's 5 attributes × 30 values each. Plus indicating range and verification scale interval per model (model_level as well, unit m³). That's a lot but it's the correct representation.\n\nAlternatively use matrix_tables for tables 4 and 5 and put indicating range/VSI in model_level too. To avoid excessive length, maybe I'll do:\n\n- matrix_tables: software table, Table 2 (full bore flow), Table 3 (reduced bore flow), Table 4 (indicating full bore), Table 5 (indicating reduced bore).\n- model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, inlet_outlet_diameter, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval.\n\nThat's heavy but faithful. Let me consider trimming: The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables