st follow structured form? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be object with value/unit_symbol... Actually the shape shown: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. In the R137 example, model_level values were shown as {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — compact. The example puts unit at the attribute level. So model_level values: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} with unit at attribute level. I'll follow the example format: values entries with model and value. Maybe include unit_symbol per value? The example doesn't. I'll keep unit at the attribute level only.\n\nGiven the example format for model_level:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}\n```\nSo values are compact {model, value}. Good.\n\nDecimal conversion: 0,125 → 0.125; 78,75 → 78.75; 39,375 → 39.375; 0,0001 → 0.0001; 15,625 → 15.625; 31,5 → 31.5; 50,4 → 50.4; 12,8 → 12.8; 3,2 → 3.2; 3,15 → 3.15; 5,04 → 5.04; 0,32 → 0.32; 0,504 → 0.504.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll include:\n1. Software identification (\"Legally relevant firmware\") — columns: software_version, crc_checksum. Rows: 10.\n2. Table 2 full bore flow characteristics — columns meter_size, inlet_outlet_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; 16 rows (including DN500, DN600 from the continuation table — merged).\n3. Table 3 reduced bore — 14 rows.\n4. Table 4 indicating device full bore — rows with meter_sizes (grouped) — columns: meter_sizes, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval.\n5. Table 5 indicating device reduced bore — 6 rows.\n\nShould column_units be included? The example schema includes column_units in the R137 example (though the yaml schema lists columns, rows, description, footnotes). The example shows \"column_units\". I'll include column_units.\n\nFor the software table, column_units: [null, null].\n\nNames: \"Software versions of legally relevant firmware\", \"Table 2: General characteristics – full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL (flow rates)\", etc.\n\nAlso there's config_level? Not really. Components? The \"indicating device\" is part of the instrument; the software is legally relevant firmware of the indicating device presumably. Could add components: [{\"role\": \"indicating device\", ...}] but the fragment doesn't strongly frame it as separate components. I might skip components. Actually the software identification table header says \"Software identification — Software 'Legally relevant firmware'\". I could record this as a component \"Legally relevant firmware\" with characteristics. But safer to keep as matrix_table + maybe a type_level characteristic? Since there are 10 versions, it's a table not a single value. Keep as matrix_table.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013 (from R49/2013 and R49-1:2013(E) references), scheme \"A\" (from number \"2013-A-NL1\"). accuracy_classes: not present in fragment. Omit.\n\ntest_reports: none in fragment. Omit.\n\nrevision_history: as above. Revision \"Initial\" → revision: \"Initial\", date 2019-05-24, changes: null or \"-\"? changes: null since \"-\" means none. Rule 8: don't fabricate; \"-\" means no changes described. I'll use null.\n\nDates: format as ISO: \"2019-05-24\", \"2021-02-04\", \"2022-05-10\", \"2022-07-07\", \"2023-02-14\", \"2023-09-22\", \"2026-08-10\".\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2026-08-10\" (revision 6 date). Hmm — is that right? The cert is revision 6; history says revision 6 on 10 August 2026. I'll include it.\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\". revision: \"6\". scheme: \"A\". project_number: \"4086814\". page_total: 7. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". oiml_issuer_id: omit (not present explicitly). \n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". No address in fragment. Include just name? I'll include issuing_authority with name \"NMi\" only. Hmm, is \"NMi\" present? Yes, \"--- NMi\" appears as header. OK.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters). Actually should I say \"Water meter\"? The fragment doesn't explicitly say \"water meter\" but R49 is \"Water meters\" — the recommendation. Category derivation from recommendation is reasonable. The schema examples list categories; \"Water meter\" fits. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"].\n- module_designation: not present → null or omit.\n- description: maybe \"Family of instruments: full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL and reduced bore type MUT2300 (electromagnetic water meters?)\" — no, don't fabricate electromagnetic. Description: null/omit. Actually I could put \"Full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL; reduced bore type MUT2300\". That's from the table titles. I'll include that as description.\n\nmodel_family: family_name: null. models: 30 entries.\n\nLet me generate model list:\n\nFull bore (type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL):\n1. \"Full bore DN32\"\n2. \"Full bore DN40\"\n3. \"Full bore DN50\"\n4. \"Full bore DN65\"\n5. \"Full bore DN80\"\n6. \"Full bore DN100\"\n7. \"Full bore DN125\"\n8. \"Full bore DN150\"\n9. \"Full bore DN200\"\n10. \"Full bore DN250\"\n11. \"Full bore DN300\"\n12. \"Full bore DN350\"\n13. \"Full bore DN400\"\n14. \"Full bore DN450\"\n15. \"Full bore DN500\"\n16. \"Full bore DN600\"\n\nReduced bore (MUT2300):\n17. \"Reduced bore DN50\" ... through \"Reduced bore DN600\": DN50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600 (14 models).\n\nHmm, model_id maybe better as \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\"? I'll use the \"Full bore DN32\" id but label includes type names. Fine.\n\nNow model_level arrays. Let me write them out.\n\nDiameter inlet/outlet (mm):\nFull: DN32:32, DN40:40, DN50:50, DN65:65, DN80:80, DN100:100, DN125:125, DN150:150, DN200:200, DN250:250, DN300:300, DN350:350, DN400:400, DN450:450, DN500:500, DN600:600.\nReduced: DN50:50, DN65:65, DN80:80, DN100:100, DN125:125, DN150:150, DN200:200, DN250:250, DN300:300, DN350:350, DN400:400, DN450:450, DN500:500, D